tr_by=hand
why=pelt#49, ruling #113: the command's other words are cd's further operands, as bash 5.3 treats them (it runs cd -- DIR a b, black-box, notes/sh23-evidence/refs/base/bash-i-autocd/p05-words); what cd does with them is pelt's cd's, which takes the first operand and leaves the rest as dash's cd does (dash cd a b goes to a, status 0, black-box, notes/sh23-evidence/refs/base2/dash-i/p13-cdwords), so DIR a b goes to DIR, status 0, where bash's own cd refuses a second operand (too many arguments, status 2) and stays. zsh 5.9 takes AUTO_CD for a lone word only: DIR a b is its plain not found, 127. Written by hand from pelt trunk's answers to the explicit cd -- DIR WORD... session (notes/sh23-evidence/handtr.py)
